  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Luis Severino gave them a chance to win; they wasted it early and never recovered.",
    "what_this_means": "The game revealed what happens when a good arm meets a fractured offense. Severino kept them hanging around through six innings on 10 hits and five runs. Respectable work on a night when nothing came easy. But the real story was the empty space. They had nine runners in scoring position and converted two of them into runs. That's not a slump. That's a locked door. The eighth inning was the only moment they broke through. Kurtz walked to start a rally that scored three. But by then San Francisco had already built a two-run cushion that held. Brent Rooker was the one bright spot, driving in three runs to keep the lineup relevant, but one man's night does not erase the rest of the order's silence. This team doesn't lose by accident. They left the game on the field in the fourth inning and spent the next four running out of time.",
    "what_to_watch": "Adrian Houser starts tonight with an ERA over 5.75, and Jeffrey Springs draws a favorable matchup against one of the league's weakest offenses. The question is whether last night's pattern. All baserunners, no damage. Repeats or finally breaks.",
